{"name":"Water Sports","hex":"#44bbcc","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"water sports","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of water sport"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/water_sports"}],"entries":[{"title":"Water Sports (EP)","blocks":[{"text":"Water Sports is an EP by DIN, released in 1993 by DOVe."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/f/f0/DIN_-_Water_Sport.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Water_Sports_(EP)","wikidata":"Q104853375"},{"title":"List of water sports","blocks":[{"text":"Water sports or aquatic sports are sports activities conducted on waterbodies and can be categorized according to the degree of immersion by the participants."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Water_sports_composite.jpg/330px-Water_sports_composite.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_water_sports","wikidata":"Q121750997"},{"title":"Water","blocks":[{"text":"Water is an inorganic compound with the chemical formula H2O. It is a transparent, tasteless, odorless, and nearly colorless chemical substance. It is the main constituent of Earth's streams, lakes, and oceans. Water is also the fluid of all known living organisms, in which it acts as a solvent. Water, being a polar molecule, undergoes strong intermolecular hydrogen bonding which is a large contributor to its physical and chemical properties. It is vital for all known forms of life, despite not providing food energy or being an organic micronutrient. Due to its presence in all organisms, its chemical stability, its worldwide abundance, and its strong polarity relative to its small molecular size, water is often referred to as the \"universal solvent\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/H2O_2D_labelled.svg/330px-H2O_2D_labelled.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Water","wikidata":"Q283"},{"title":"Sport","blocks":[{"text":"A sport is a physical activity or game, often competitive and organised, that maintains or improves physical ability and skills. Sport may provide enjoyment to participants and entertainment to spectators. The number of participants in a particular sport can vary from hundreds of people to a single individual."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Youth-soccer-indiana.jpg/330px-Youth-soccer-indiana.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sport","wikidata":"Q349"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Water-3D-balls-A.png/500px-Water-3D-balls-A.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Water-3D-balls-A.png/500px-Water-3D-balls-A.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ball-and-stick model of a water molecule","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWater-3D-balls-A.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Water_drop_001.jpg/500px-Water_drop_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Water_drop_001.jpg/1280px-Water_drop_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Water_drop_001.jpg/500px-Water_drop_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A drop of water falling toward water in a glass","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWater_drop_001.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Pillow_basalt_crop_l.j